QUESTIONS = [
    # CH35 L3 (+2)
    {"text":"Consider the following about Article 323A and the principle of 'functional independence' of adjudicatory tribunals:\n1. The SC in L. Chandra Kumar held that a tribunal performing judicial functions must have functional independence from the executive.\n2. This means even administrative tribunals must follow rules of natural justice.\n3. Functional independence requires that members cannot be transferred or removed by the executive at will.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three correctly capture the functional independence principle: judicial-like functions require natural justice, independence from executive influence, and security of tenure. All three flow from L. Chandra Kumar and subsequent cases.","chapter_number":35,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the evolution of 'expert tribunals' in India:\n1. The rationale for creating specialised tribunals is that technical domains (tax, environment, telecom) require domain expertise that generalist judges lack.\n2. The SC has held that tribunals with ONLY technical (non-lawyer) members are constitutionally invalid for adjudicating legal rights.\n3. A bench with both a retired judge and a domain expert fully satisfies the constitutional requirement for tribunals.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three are correct. The expert-rationale (1), the invalidity of all-technical benches for legal rights (2), and the validity of mixed benches (3) are established in the Madras Bar Association line of cases.","chapter_number":35,"level":3},

    # CH47 L1 (+15)
    {"text":"Under the Fifth Schedule, the Governor's annual report on Scheduled Areas is submitted to whom?","options":["The State Legislature","The President of India","The Ministry of Tribal Affairs","The National Commission for Scheduled Tribes"],"correct_answer":"The President of India","explanation":"Paragraph 3 of the Fifth Schedule requires the Governor of each State having Scheduled Areas to make a report to the President regarding the administration of Scheduled Areas annually or at such other time as the President may direct.","chapter_number":47,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which body was created by the 89th Constitutional Amendment (2003) specifically to safeguard the rights of Scheduled Tribes?","options":["National Commission for Backward Classes","National Commission for Scheduled Castes","National Commission for Scheduled Tribes","Tribal Welfare Council of India"],"correct_answer":"National Commission for Scheduled Tribes","explanation":"The 89th Amendment Act 2003 bifurcated the single National Commission for SCs and STs into two separate bodies — the National Commission for Scheduled Castes and the National Commission for Scheduled Tribes — for more focused attention.","chapter_number":47,"level":1},
    {"text":"The National Commission for Scheduled Tribes (NCST) is a constitutional body established under which Article?","options":["Article 338","Article 338A","Article 338B","Article 341"],"correct_answer":"Article 338A","explanation":"Article 338A was inserted by the 89th Constitutional Amendment Act, 2003 to establish the NCST as an independent constitutional body to protect the rights of Scheduled Tribes.","chapter_number":47,"level":1},
    {"text":"PESA (1996) mandates that Gram Sabhas in Scheduled Areas must be consulted before acquiring land for development projects. This is to protect which principle?","options":["Right to Property (Art 300A)","Free, Prior and Informed Consent (FPIC) of tribal communities","Right to Equality (Art 14)","Freedom of Movement (Art 19(1)(d))"],"correct_answer":"Free, Prior and Informed Consent (FPIC) of tribal communities","explanation":"PESA's consultation and consent requirement embodies the international principle of Free, Prior and Informed Consent (FPIC) for indigenous/tribal communities recognised under UNDRIP and ILO Convention 169.","chapter_number":47,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which of the following states does NOT have any area designated as 'Scheduled Area' under the Fifth Schedule?","options":["Jharkhand","Chhattisgarh","Bihar","Madhya Pradesh"],"correct_answer":"Bihar","explanation":"Bihar does not have any Scheduled Areas under the Fifth Schedule. Jharkhand (carved from Bihar in 2000), Chhattisgarh, and Madhya Pradesh all have Fifth Schedule Scheduled Areas.","chapter_number":47,"level":1},
    {"text":"Under PESA, which of the following powers is specifically vested in the Gram Sabha of Scheduled Areas?","options":["Power to levy municipal taxes","Power to manage village markets and prevent alienation of land","Power to appoint panchayat members","Power to override state legislation on forest areas"],"correct_answer":"Power to manage village markets and prevent alienation of land","explanation":"PESA empowers Gram Sabhas in Scheduled Areas to manage village markets, control money lending, prevent land alienation, manage minor forest produce, and oversee minor water bodies. These are key grassroots economic powers.","chapter_number":47,"level":1},
    {"text":"The Sixth Schedule under Article 244(2) applies to tribal areas in which states?","options":["All states with tribal population above 25%","Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura, Mizoram","Nagaland, Manipur, Arunachal Pradesh, Sikkim","All North-Eastern states except Assam"],"correct_answer":"Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura, Mizoram","explanation":"Article 244(2) and the Sixth Schedule apply specifically to the tribal areas of Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura, and Mizoram. The other NE states are governed by different constitutional provisions.","chapter_number":47,"level":1},
    {"text":"Under the Sixth Schedule, who has the power to alter the boundaries of Autonomous Districts?","options":["The Parliament by law","The Governor by public notification","The President by order","The ADC by resolution passed by 2/3 majority"],"correct_answer":"The Governor by public notification","explanation":"Paragraph 1(2) and 1(3) of the Sixth Schedule vest with the Governor the power to organise new autonomous districts, increase or decrease the area, unite two districts, alter the name or boundaries — by order/notification.","chapter_number":47,"level":1},
    {"text":"The Samatha judgment (1997) is best understood in the context of which constitutional issue?","options":["Tribal women's rights in Scheduled Areas","Mining leases and transfer of tribal land in Fifth Schedule areas to non-tribal private companies","ADC elections in the Sixth Schedule areas","Extension of PESA to urban tribal areas"],"correct_answer":"Mining leases and transfer of tribal land in Fifth Schedule areas to non-tribal private companies","explanation":"Samatha v. State of AP (1997) held that the Andhra Pradesh government could not grant mining leases in Scheduled Areas to non-tribal private companies. Only tribals or state government entities could operate in such areas.","chapter_number":47,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which of the following is a characteristic unique to ADCs (Autonomous District Councils) under the Sixth Schedule compared to PRIs under the 73rd Amendment?","options":["ADCs have NO legislative powers — only executive powers","ADCs can make laws on certain subjects within their area that override state laws, subject to Governor's assent","ADCs are directly under the Union Government, not the State Government","ADCs have concurrent jurisdiction with the High Court"],"correct_answer":"ADCs can make laws on certain subjects within their area that override state laws, subject to Governor's assent","explanation":"Sixth Schedule ADCs are unique in having quasi-legislative powers — they can make regulations/laws on specified subjects (land, forests, moneylending, etc.). These are subject to the Governor's assent and can override state laws to that extent.","chapter_number":47,"level":1},
    {"text":"Tribal communities in scheduled areas have the constitutional right to manage which of the following under PESA?","options":["Urban civic services like water supply and streetlights","Minor forest produce (MFP) as community resource","All forest land without exception","Agricultural taxation in their villages"],"correct_answer":"Minor forest produce (MFP) as community resource","explanation":"PESA explicitly recognises the Gram Sabha's right over Minor Forest Produce (tendu leaves, bamboo, herbs, etc.) in Scheduled Areas. This was a crucial recognition of the traditional tribal economy.","chapter_number":47,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which of the following correctly describes the difference between 'Autonomous District,' 'Autonomous Region,' and 'Autonomous Council' under the Sixth Schedule?","options":["A District can contain one or more Regions if one tribal community is dominant; all have separate ADCs","They are the same — the names are used interchangeably","An Autonomous Region is larger than Autonomous District","An Autonomous Council is elected; an ADC is appointed"],"correct_answer":"A District can contain one or more Regions if one tribal community is dominant; all have separate ADCs","explanation":"Under Para 1(1) of the Sixth Schedule, an Autonomous District may contain Autonomous Regions if different tribes occupy different parts. Each Region has its own Regional Council while the District has a District Council.","chapter_number":47,"level":1},
    {"text":"The Second Schedule to the Constitution is different from the Fifth Schedule. The Second Schedule relates to:","options":["Emoluments and allowances of constitutional functionaries","Scheduled Tribes notified by the President","Functions to be performed by Municipalities","Union-State legislative relations"],"correct_answer":"Emoluments and allowances of constitutional functionaries","explanation":"The Second Schedule contains provisions relating to emoluments, allowances, privileges, and conditions of service of the President, Governors, Speakers, Judges, CAG, etc. It is unrelated to tribal administration (Fifth Schedule).","chapter_number":47,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which ministry administers the Fifth Schedule and tribal welfare schemes at the Central Government level?","options":["Ministry of Home Affairs","Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment","Ministry of Tribal Affairs","Ministry of Rural Development"],"correct_answer":"Ministry of Tribal Affairs","explanation":"The Ministry of Tribal Affairs (MoTA), created in 1999 as a separate ministry, is the nodal ministry for the administration of Fifth Schedule areas, tribal welfare, and tribal-specific schemes like PM Janjati Vikas Mission.","chapter_number":47,"level":1},
    {"text":"The 'Minor Forest Produce' (MFP) recognised as tribal community resource under PESA includes:","options":["Timber trees","Bamboo, tendu leaves, honey, medicinal plants, tusser silk","Large timber reserved forest produce","Coffee and tea grown by tribal farmers"],"correct_answer":"Bamboo, tendu leaves, honey, medicinal plants, tusser silk","explanation":"MFP under PESA includes bamboo, tendu leaves, honey, wax, lac, mahua, medicinal plants, and other non-timber forest produce traditionally collected and used by tribal communities. Large timber is reserved forest produce under the Forest Act.","chapter_number":47,"level":1},

    # CH47 L2 (+15)
    {"text":"Consider the following about the implementation gap between PESA's text and practice:\n1. Several states revised their Panchayati Raj Acts to comply with PESA but kept key Gram Sabha powers weak through bureaucratic procedures.\n2. District Collectors in Scheduled Areas often bypass Gram Sabha consent requirements.\n3. The Central Government has no mechanism to compel state compliance with PESA.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three correctly capture documented PESA implementation failures. Weak state acts, administrative bypass, and the absence of enforcement mechanisms are all well-documented critiques.","chapter_number":47,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the Sixth Schedule and elections:\n1. The State Election Commission conducts ADC elections.\n2. ADC members serve a 5-year term.\n3. Reserved seats for SCs are mandated in ADCs under the Sixth Schedule.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — the Sixth Schedule does not mandate SC reservations in ADCs; it is focused on tribal self-governance. Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":47,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the National Commission for Scheduled Tribes (NCST) under Article 338A:\n1. The NCST investigates and monitors safeguards for STs under the Constitution and other laws.\n2. The NCST can recommend prosecution in cases of tribal rights violation.\n3. The NCST's recommendations are binding on the Central and State Governments.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — NCST recommendations are advisory, not binding. Statements 1 and 2 correctly reflect NCST's constitutional mandate under Article 338A.","chapter_number":47,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the Sixth Schedule's judicial provisions:\n1. ADCs can constitute courts to try tribal members for offences against customary tribal law.\n2. ADC courts can impose punishment up to a specified limit, including imprisonment.\n3. Appeals from ADC courts lie directly to the Supreme Court.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — appeals from ADC courts go to the High Court (not directly to the SC). Statements 1 and 2 correctly describe the judicial powers of ADCs under the Sixth Schedule.","chapter_number":47,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the historical basis of tribal administration under the Constitution:\n1. The Fifth and Sixth Schedules draw from the colonial-era 'excluded areas' and 'partially excluded areas' under the Government of India Act 1935.\n2. The Constituent Assembly chose to retain special administration for tribal areas rather than fully integrate them immediately.\n3. The colonial model of 'excluded areas' fully preserved tribal customs without any interference.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — the colonial 'excluded areas' model was paternalistic administration, not genuine preservation of tribal self-governance. Statements 1 and 2 correctly trace the historical roots.","chapter_number":47,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about 'conversion of forests' in Scheduled Areas under PESA:\n1. PESA requires Gram Sabha consent before converting forest land to non-forest use.\n2. The Forest Conservation Act 1980's diversion process must be followed independently of PESA.\n3. Both PESA and FCA processes are required cumulatively before any forest diversion in Scheduled Areas.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three are correct. PESA's Gram Sabha consent, FCA's forest diversion approval, and (if applicable) FRA's community rights recognition — all are required cumulatively in Scheduled Areas.","chapter_number":47,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the extension of reservations to Scheduled Tribes:\n1. Article 330 reserves seats for STs in the House of the People (Lok Sabha).\n2. Article 332 reserves seats for STs in State Legislative Assemblies.\n3. The reservation for STs in Parliament and State Assemblies originally had a sunset clause of 10 years and has been extended periodically.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three are correct. Arts 330 and 332 provide for ST reservations in Parliament and state assemblies. The original sunset period of 10 years has been extended repeatedly by constitutional amendments (most recently by the 104th Amendment to 2030).","chapter_number":47,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about tribal land alienation and related constitutional safeguards:\n1. Article 244 and the Fifth Schedule empower Governors to prevent land transfer from tribals to non-tribals.\n2. Land reform laws in many Fifth Schedule states provide that land transferred away from tribals is deemed void or subject to restoration.\n3. The constitutional protections have been fully effective — land alienation has been eliminated in all Fifth Schedule states.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — land alienation continues despite protections; implementation failures are well-documented across all Fifth Schedule states. Statements 1 and 2 correctly describe the legal framework.","chapter_number":47,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the Fifth Schedule's provision on 'Regulation of Land':\n1. The Governor may by regulation prohibit or restrict the transfer of land by or among members of Scheduled Tribes.\n2. This power overrides conflicting state land laws in Scheduled Areas.\n3. This power also overrides central laws like the Land Acquisition Act.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is contested — the Land Acquisition framework (now LARR Act 2013) has its own constitutional basis and central laws are not automatically overridden by Fifth Schedule regulations. Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":47,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the 'Tribal Sub-Plan' (now PM-JANMAN) approach to tribal development:\n1. The Tribal Sub-Plan was an administrative device, not a constitutional requirement.\n2. PESA mandates a separate tribal sub-plan for Scheduled Areas as part of district planning.\n3. PM-JANMAN replaced the Tribal Sub-Plan framework after 2023.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect — PESA does not mandate a separate sub-plan; it was an administrative policy. Statements 1 and 3 are correct — Tribal Sub-Plan was policy-based and PM-JANMAN replaced it.","chapter_number":47,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the relationship between Article 15(4) and Fifth Schedule:\n1. Article 15(4) enables special provisions for advancement of STs — the Fifth Schedule is one such provision.\n2. Reservations for STs in government jobs (Article 16(4)) are constitutionally distinct from Fifth Schedule land/governance protections.\n3. Fifth Schedule rights can be waived by an individual tribal member if they choose to sell land to non-tribals.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — Fifth Schedule protections are community-based constitutional safeguards, not personal rights that can be individually waived. Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":47,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about Article 338A (NCST) and its investigative powers:\n1. The NCST has all powers of a civil court for summoning witnesses and examining documents.\n2. The NCST can investigate allegations of tribal rights violations suo motu.\n3. Central and State Governments are required to consult the NCST on all policy matters affecting STs.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three are correct under Article 338A. The NCST has civil court powers, suo motu jurisdiction, and mandatory consultation rights. All three correctly capture the constitutional mandate.","chapter_number":47,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about Biodiversity hot-spots in Fifth Schedule areas:\n1. Most of India's biodiversity hotspots overlap with tribal Scheduled Areas.\n2. Conservation and tribal rights have often been in conflict in Scheduled Areas.\n3. The Biological Diversity Act 2002 fully resolves the conflict between conservation and tribal rights.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — the tension between conservation and tribal rights continues; the Biological Diversity Act provides for Biodiversity Management Committees but does not fully resolve conflicts. Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":47,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the status of Schedule Tribes in the Constitution:\n1. The President specifies the list of Scheduled Tribes by order under Article 342.\n2. Parliament can include or exclude communities from the ST list by law.\n3. A community once listed as ST cannot be de-notified without constitutional amendment.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect — Parliament can modify the ST list by ordinary law (not constitutional amendment). Statements 1 and 2 are correct under Articles 342 and 342(2).","chapter_number":47,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the following about the Autonomous District Councils and their financial powers:\n1. ADCs have the power to levy and collect taxes from persons resident in their areas.\n2. ADC budgets must be approved by the state government.\n3. ADCs receive grants from both Central and State Governments in addition to their own tax revenues.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three are correct about ADC financial powers under the Sixth Schedule. ADCs levy taxes (para 8), budgets need state approval, and grants flow from both Centre and State.","chapter_number":47,"level":2},
]
